well, actually my idea was a little bit different: the mechanism as you
did works perfectly fine, so I would not modify a lot the approach. The
underlying idea, as I understood it, is that one lets the user define
himself all the colors he will want to be mapped into spotcolor. That
means the question of what is p=0.5 of Pantone x is left to the user,
and the metapost() and meta.color() only see that the defined colors are
matched afterwards to the spotcolor specs, by defining the relevant
\definecolor.

It is clear an (highly) interesting extension would be to be able only
to specify rgb color of one spotcolor, and let metapost() find out what
is the corresponding rgb values... But if we say user defines himself
how the spotcolor and his tints are shown in the screen, then there are
only a few modifications needed to make the use of the tints available....
Maybe by:

-What format.metap_color () does is perfectly fine: export color, to
list. But then problem is it gets every time its own definition... In
one way it would describe what format.metap_tiny() would ideally do:
define color, export to list... The best in my opinion would then to
allow for p and color ref
-Add a metap_spotcolor: is not matched by any actual color, just leads
to def of the spotcolor

-modifying format.metap to:
     -write only once \definecolor[PantoneBlue][c=1, m=0.58,y=0,k=0.29]
for parent color
     -write for every metap color line
\definespotcolor[PantoneBlueHundredSpot][PantoneBlue][p=1,e=Pantone 294 C]

I don't know if I am very clear... but do you understand the approach I
am describing? Does it makes sense to you?

Thanks!!

